  "provider_query": "English Wikipedia HTTP article: the default TCP port",
  "answer": "The English Wikipedia **HTTP** article states that unencrypted HTTP typically uses **TCP port 80**.\n\nRelevant results:\n\n1. **HTTP** — TCP/IP connections use well-known ports, typically **port 80** if unencrypted (port 443 if encrypted). Prior to HTTP/3, TCP is the standard transport.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP\n\n2. **Port (computer networking)** — By default, HTTP uses **port 80** and HTTPS uses port 443; a non-default port must be written in the URL (e.g. `:8080`).  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_(computer_networking)\n\n3. **List of TCP and UDP port numbers** — **Port 80** is assigned to Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP); HTTP/1.x and HTTP/2 use TCP. HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_TCP_and_UDP_port_numbers\n\n4. **URL** — A browser usually dereferences an HTTP URL with a request to the host **on port 80 by default**.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/URL\n\n5. **Port 80** (disambiguation) — TCP port 80 is most often used by Hypertext Transfer Protocol.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_80\n\n6. **Port (computer networking)** — Well-known ports table lists **80** (and 8080) for Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP).  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_(computer_networking)\n\n7. **HTTP** — Example request–response is shown for a server at `www.example.com`, **port 80**.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP\n\n8. **List of TCP and UDP port numbers** — **Port 443** is HTTPS (HTTP over TLS); HTTP/1.x and 2 use TCP there as well.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_TCP_and_UDP_port_numbers",
